<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Additional Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection of Hyperparameter <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We first provide accuracy comparisons under three settings: baseline (<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), task-agnostic (<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>), and task-specific (<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> determined by tasks). The results are shown in Table <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table A15 ‣ B.4 Additional Ablation Studies ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ VL-SAE: Interpreting and Enhancing Vision-Language Alignment with a Unified Concept Set" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A15</span></a>.
First, the task-agnostic scheme performs better than the baseline (72.4% v.s. 72.2% on ViT-L, and 77.2% v.s. 76.9% on ViT-H). This indicates that <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> does not need to be task-specific for performance improvement and can generalize across tasks.
However, these tasks originate from different domains and exhibit distinct preferences for hyperparameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, as evidenced by the best performance of the task-specific scheme. If the objective is to achieve optimal performance, the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values of each task should be determined individually,
To determine the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> value of each task, we conduct experiments with multiple values <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> at intervals of 0.1 and select the one that performs the best. Note that the cost of conducting multiple experiments is very low. For instance, when using the Food-101 dataset, each experiment on a single NVIDIA 4090 GPU takes only approximately 90 seconds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T15" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table A15: </span>Ablation of hyperparameter <math id="A2.T15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.3 Interpreting and Enhancing the Vision-Language Alignment ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ VL-SAE: Interpreting and Enhancing Vision-Language Alignment with a Unified Concept Set" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T15.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
